# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Rust build
/target
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT here. This is a binary crate, so the lock file
# is committed: a release build should resolve to the same dependency versions
# that were tested, not to whatever is newest on the day someone clones it.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ build artefacts
# Inno Setup writes the installer here (see packaging/inno/sonus.iss).
/dist
# `choco pack` output.
/packaging/choco/*.nupkg

# Raw pixel dumps from `--dump-icons` and `--render-panel`. build.ps1 writes
# these under target/, but a hand-run with a different path should not leave
# multi-megabyte buffers lying around in the tree.
*.rgba
*.rgba.size

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the site
# docs/ is the GitHub Pages source and IS committed, images included: they are
# generated by the app rather than screenshotted (build.ps1 -Shot / -Icons), but
# Pages has no build step and needs them checked in.
#
# Contact sheets are development-only and land in target/, so nothing to add.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- tooling
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~

# Machine-specific tool permissions. A shared .claude/settings.json, if one is
# ever added, stays tracked.
.claude/settings.local.json

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- Windows
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- macOS
# build.sh assembles dist/Sonus.app (already covered by /dist) and can wrap it in
# a .dmg there too. Finder litter is the only thing left to keep out.
.DS_Store

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- runtime
# Nothing the app writes at runtime lives in the repo -- config.json, state.json
# and sonus.log all go to %APPDATA%\Sonus on Windows, to
# ~/Library/Application Support/Sonus on macOS and to ~/.config/sonus on Linux.
# Noted here only so the next person does not go looking for them.
